I can use the core premise, but I’m going to fictionalize the populations rather than make real ethnic or religious groups the subjects of an imposed deprivation experiment. That actually makes the satire cleaner: Cage can pitch “chronological zoning” to the League of Doom as if he’s solved geopolitics with a museum exhibit.

The League of Doom had rejected Nicolas Cage’s proposal three times before allowing him into the conference room.

The first rejection said:

TOO AMBITIOUS.

The second said:

UNCLEAR GOVERNANCE MODEL.

The third simply said:

WHY IS NICOLAS CAGE INVOLVED?

Cage considered all three encouraging.

Now he stood beneath an enormous glowing map of the world while the assembled supervillains watched from a horseshoe-shaped table.

Lex Luthor sat at the center.

Sinestro floated slightly above his chair for no apparent reason.

Black Manta was visibly annoyed by the room temperature.

The Riddler had brought his own whiteboard.

And Bizarro had somehow been given voting rights.

Cage clicked a remote.

The world map divided into brightly colored regions.

Lex Luthor narrowed his eyes.

“What exactly are we looking at?”

Cage smiled.

“The end of synchronized civilization.”

Silence.

The Riddler leaned forward.

“That’s a good opening.”

“Thank you.”

Cage clicked again.

One region became labeled:

ZONE A — 1880

Another:

ZONE B — 1927

Another:

ZONE C — 350 BCE

Another:

ZONE D — PRESENT DAY

And one tiny island flashed:

ZONE E — 2049

Black Manta said, “Why does the island get the future?”

Cage ignored him.

Lex folded his hands.

“Explain.”

Cage walked slowly toward the map.

“We have made a catastrophic assumption.”

Sinestro asked, “Which is?”

“That everyone has to live in the same century.”

The room became extremely quiet.

Cage continued.

“Why?”

Nobody answered.

“Why does a fisherman in the Republic of Talassar require cloud computing?”

The Riddler raised a finger.

“He may enjoy cloud computing.”

“That is not the point.”

“It seems relevant.”

Cage clicked the remote.

A village appeared on screen.

Ox carts.

Stone buildings.

A water wheel.

A man making pottery.

Cage gestured toward it.

“This is Talassar.”

Lex said, “Fictional country?”

“Yes.”

“Good.”

Cage looked briefly relieved.

“We remove modern infrastructure.”

Black Manta tilted his helmet.

“All of it?”

“No. Selectively.”

Cage’s expression became intense.

“Hospitals stay.”

Lex nodded slightly.

“Water purification stays.”

Another nod.

“Antibiotics stay.”

Sinestro said, “Sensible.”

“TikTok goes.”

The entire room murmured approvingly.

Cage continued.

“Internal combustion engines? Gone.”

“Electric grids?”

“Localized.”

“Satellites?”

“Only the administrators.”

Lex’s eyebrow rose.

“Administrators?”

Cage clicked again.

A new diagram appeared:

CHRONOLOGICAL LIAISONS

Cage pointed.

“The only people who know the full technological reality.”

The Riddler stared at the diagram.

“So… wizards.”

“No.”

“They know hidden knowledge.”

“Yes.”

“They possess technology the population doesn’t understand.”

“Yes.”

“They travel between worlds.”

“Administrative zones.”

“They manipulate reality from the perspective of ordinary people.”

Cage hesitated.

“Fine. Wizards.”

The Riddler leaned back, satisfied.

—

Cage moved to the next region.

THE KINGDOM OF ORION — APPROX. 900 BCE

The screen showed enormous temples, bronze weapons, irrigation canals, scribes, astronomical observatories, and ceremonial processions.

Sinestro said:

“You intend to convince them this is actually 900 BCE?”

“Approximately.”

“Approximately?”

“Calendars become complicated.”

Lex Luthor rubbed his chin.

“And who maintains the illusion?”

Cage smiled.

“Continuity officers.”

The Riddler laughed.

“That is absolutely a wizard.”

“They have tablets.”

“Stone tablets?”

Cage clicked.

A continuity officer appeared wearing ceremonial robes.

He was holding an iPad inside a carved wooden case.

Everyone stared.

Black Manta finally said:

“That is the funniest thing you’ve shown us.”

The officer approached a farmer.

On his hidden tablet was:

WEATHER SATELLITE FORECAST: STORM IN 18 HOURS

The officer turned solemnly toward the farmer.

“The sky spirits warn of rain.”

Sinestro slowly nodded.

“Oh.”

Cage pointed triumphantly.

“Yes.”

“You’ve reinvented priesthood using meteorology.”

“Precisely.”

Lex looked disturbed.

Not morally.

Professionally.

“You understand this creates an information aristocracy.”

Cage nodded.

“Correct.”

“You say that like it’s a feature.”

“No.”

Cage paused.

“I say it like it’s going to happen regardless, so I put it on the slide.”

That earned him his first genuine look of respect from Lex Luthor.

—

The next slide showed a coastal society living with roughly 1600s technology.

Sailing ships.

Printing presses.

Early scientific societies.

Mechanical clocks.

Cage explained:

“They receive telescopes.”

The Riddler said, “Why?”

“Because telescopes are excellent.”

“Fair.”

“Printing presses.”

“Obviously.”

“Gunpowder?”

Cage stopped.

“No.”

Black Manta said, “Why not?”

“Because every time humans discover something useful they eventually point it at another human.”

Lex nodded.

“Also fair.”

Cage clicked again.

Another region appeared.

This one had telephones.

Automobiles.

Jazz clubs.

Radio towers.

Art Deco buildings.

Sinestro leaned forward.

“What year?”

“1927 forever.”

“Forever?”

“Culturally.”

“Why 1927?”

Cage stared at him.

“Look at the architecture.”

Nobody argued.

—

Then came the controversial zone.

A huge central continent labeled:

THE ANCIENT RESTORATION PROJECT

The screen showed reconstructed cities based on archaeological and historical scholarship.

Canals.

Markets.

Temples.

Mathematics schools.

Agricultural estates.

Bronze workshops.

Cage became noticeably excited.

“This one is fascinating.”

Lex said, “Of course it is.”

“The people are told that their civilization is living at the beginning of history.”

The Riddler frowned.

“But they’re modern people.”

“Initially.”

“They remember smartphones.”

“Initially.”

The room went still.

Lex’s voice sharpened.

“What do you mean initially?”

Cage paused.

Then realized he had wandered into the part of the presentation that sounded dramatically more evil when spoken aloud.

“We phase it generationally.”

“How?”

“Education.”

Sinestro leaned forward.

“Propaganda?”

Cage looked uncomfortable.

“Extremely committed historical reenactment.”

Lex stared at him.

“Propaganda.”

“Yes.”

The Riddler stood.

“Now we’re actually doing villain stuff.”

Cage pointed at him.

“That is exactly the problem.”

“What?”

“You’re enjoying it.”

“We’re the League of Doom.”

“I thought this was Super Friends.”

“Wrong building.”

Cage slowly looked around.

At Lex Luthor.

At Sinestro.

At Black Manta.

At the giant skull motif behind the conference table.

“Oh.”

Lex said, “You genuinely didn’t know?”

“There are a surprising number of halls.”

—

Cage reached for the remote.

“Okay. Important revision.”

He deleted half the slide.

“Participation must be voluntary.”

Groans erupted around the room.

Black Manta slammed a fist on the table.

“Oh, come on.”

Cage ignored him.

“People can leave.”

More groans.

“People can know the outside world exists.”

Lex frowned.

“Then the illusion collapses.”

“Good.”

“Good?”

Cage nodded.

“Otherwise this is just an enormous prison with excellent production design.”

The Riddler slowly sat down.

“That is annoyingly correct.”

Cage changed the title:

CHRONOLOGICAL AUTONOMY ZONES

Sinestro read it aloud.

“So now people choose their technological era?”

“Yes.”

“And can move between them?”

“Yes.”

“Then what is the experiment?”

Cage smiled.

“Now we actually learn something.”

Lex leaned back.

Cage walked toward the map.

“For centuries, we’ve treated technological progress as one directional.”

He drew an arrow:

MORE TECHNOLOGY → BETTER

Then crossed it out.

“What if that is wrong?”

The room quieted again.

“What if some technologies improve human life and others merely increase throughput?”

Cage clicked.

Social media disappeared from one region.

Then cars.

Then television.

Then industrial agriculture.

Other things remained.

Vaccines.

Sanitation.

Modern surgery.

Emergency communications.

Scientific knowledge.

Cage said:

“We stop treating history like a package deal.”

Lex’s eyes narrowed.

Cage continued.

“A society could choose 1850 transportation with 2026 medicine.”

Another click.

“Ancient urban density with modern water sanitation.”

Another.

“1920s architecture with renewable electricity.”

Another.

“Medieval craftsmanship with CNC machines hidden in municipal workshops.”

The Riddler raised his hand.

“That’s cheating.”

Cage looked offended.

“No. That’s civilization.”

—

Soon the villains were arguing.

Sinestro wanted one zone organized entirely around discipline.

Black Manta wanted an aquatic zone.

The Riddler wanted a city where every public service required solving a puzzle.

Lex wanted a hyper-efficient technocracy.

Bizarro proposed:

“Future city with no future!”

Everyone ignored him.

Cage stood at the center of the chaos, delighted.

Then he noticed something.

The map.

All the boundaries.

All the eras.

All the assumptions.

He slowly lowered his remote.

“Wait.”

Nobody heard him.

“Wait.”

Still arguing.

Cage shouted:

“WAIT!”

Silence.

He pointed to the map.

“We’re doing it again.”

Lex frowned.

“Doing what?”

“Assuming the unit is geography.”

The Riddler’s eyes lit up.

“Oh.”

Cage began clicking frantically.

The borders disappeared.

The world became layered.

A city appeared where one neighborhood used bicycles and streetcars.

Another used autonomous vehicles.

One district prohibited screens in public.

Another was saturated with augmented reality.

One school taught primarily through apprenticeships.

Another used AI tutors.

One community built with stone and timber.

Another printed houses robotically.

Cage whispered:

“It doesn’t have to be countries.”

Lex stood.

“It could be institutions.”

“Families.”

“Schools.”

“Companies.”

“Neighborhoods.”

“Individuals.”

Cage looked at him.

Lex looked at Cage.

For one terrifying moment they completely understood each other.

Then Cage said:

“Don’t make this evil.”

Lex sat down.

“No promises.”

—

Three months later, the first pilot opened.

Not a country.

Not a prison.

Not a secret experiment.

A town.

Its residents had voted on their technological constitution.

Modern medicine.

Modern sanitation.

Modern emergency services.

No private cars downtown.

No algorithmic advertising.

Minimal social media.

Mechanical public clocks.

Streetcars.

Markets.

Libraries.

Workshops.

AI available in designated buildings instead of constantly carried in everyone’s pocket.

The architecture looked roughly 1910.

The medical clinic looked 2030.

The bakery looked 1780.

The municipal data center looked like something from science fiction.

Nobody pretended it was actually the past.

They simply borrowed what they wanted from it.

Nicolas Cage visited six months later.

He stood in the town square beside Lex Luthor, who was wearing a hat because the town council had passed an ordinance stating that “men look better in hats.”

Lex hated the ordinance.

Cage loved it.

A streetcar rolled past.

Children were playing in a fountain.

A delivery drone briefly crossed overhead, descended behind a brick building, and vanished.

Cage smiled.

Lex said:

“You know this is no longer a supervillain plan.”

“Yes.”

“It’s municipal policy.”

“Yes.”

“You’ve ruined everything.”

Cage looked at him.

“No.”

He gestured at the town.

“I selectively technologically regressed supervillainy.”

Lex stared.

Cage smiled.

“You’re welcome.”

Across the plaza, Bizarro had opened a store.

Its sign read:

ANTIQUES FROM TOMORROW

Cage stopped.

“What does he sell?”

Lex sighed.

“New appliances that he damages and then claims haven’t been invented yet.”

Cage stared through the window.

Bizarro was selling a scratched microwave labeled:

ANCIENT FOOD LASER — 2047

A tourist bought it.

Cage nodded slowly.

“That’s actually pretty good.”

Lex looked at him.

“Do not encourage him.”

Cage looked around at the strange little town—a place simultaneously old, new, borrowed, invented, nostalgic, experimental, and entirely present.

Then he smiled.

“Maybe civilization doesn’t move through time.”

Lex sighed.

“I know I’m going to regret asking.”

Cage pointed around them.

“Maybe time moves through civilization.”

Lex stared at him.

“That means nothing.”

Cage thought about it.

“Not yet.”

And somewhere across town, the clock tower struck noon using a mechanical bell controlled by a computer no citizen had bothered to hide.

Because it turned out the interesting experiment wasn’t convincing people they lived in the past.

It was letting them decide how much of the future they actually wanted.

That version keeps your interesting kernel: technology becomes modular rather than a single historical package. The funny turn is that Cage walks into the League of Doom with an authoritarian civilization-scale experiment and accidentally talks himself into voluntary technological constitutions—which is arguably the much more interesting thought experiment anyway.